  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4a/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_25.png",
  "plot_summary": "Reed urges Ben to drink a formula he's accidently stumbled upon that will permanently change him back to Ben Grimm, but the Thing smashes it because Alicia likes him as the Thing.&nbsp; He only wants a formula that will allow him to change back and forth!&nbsp; Alicia then arrives with a newspaper that announces that the Avengers have returned to the US after replacing the Hulk with Captain America.&nbsp; But what happened to the Hulk?\n\nAt that moment, out in New Mexico, the Hulk catches a ride on a truck (after demanding its drivers to \"Let me ride in your truck, and I'll let you live!\").&nbsp; But as they drive away, he suddenly reverts back to \"Bob\" Banner and hops out at the next stop, \"heading for the hills!\"\n\nNot far away finds the Avengers examining some of the wreckage left behind by the Hulk's takeover of the truck.&nbsp; Captain America has now joined their ranks, and they've travelled out to New Mexico in search of the Hulk.&nbsp; But the trail runs cold, and they're forced to return to New York City.\n\nMeanwhile, the scene then shifts to the Avengers, with Captain America now, who are still looking for him, having followed the trail to New Mexico.&nbsp; But the trail goes cold, and they head back to NYC (in a helicopter I might add, which would <i>never</i> make the trip all the way from there).&nbsp; Hulk sees in the newspaper that the Avengers have replaced him with Captain America, so he decides to head back to NYC to \"destroy the Avengers forever!\"&nbsp; He makes \"powerful, distance-swallowing leaps\" until he arrives 24 hours later.\n\n\n(<i>wow, that's two appearances in a row where Stan flubbed the name - multiple times I might add</i>)!&nbsp; After changing once again to the Hulk, he heads to Dr. Banner's underground hiding place and smashes any equipment that he thinks might change him back to \"Bob\" Banner.\n\n\nFinally we return to the Baxter Building, where suddenly Reed has taken ill, so Johnny takes the Fantasti-Car to go fetch the doctor, but he spots the Hulk below, smashing up a building (an \"old abandoned building\" of course).&nbsp; He takes off as the Torch to investigate, but is bested by the Hulk, who leaves him since he's actually looking for the Avengers.&nbsp; (<i>Uh...what happened to the Fantasti-Car?&nbsp; Does it just hover in the air waiting?</i>)\n\nThe Hulk's rampage causes the police commissioner to summon the FF for help, but Ben says that Reed is sick.&nbsp; Johnny is gone, but Reed, who can't go himself, urges Ben and Sue to go after the Hulk, warning Ben that the Hulk is far stronger than he - he must use his wits!&nbsp; They get to the Hulk just as Johnny recovers, and Sue tries to protect him from the Hulk with her force shield, but the strain is too much, and she loses consciousness.\n\nThe Thing finally confronts the Hulk face-to-face (<i>remember, they've met before in [LINK:1962:FF_12|FF #12]</i>), but the Hulk is not interested in the Thing - he wants only the Avengers and will smash through anything that gets in his way!&nbsp; So be it - \"the two titans confront each other...heralding the start of one of the greatest hand-to-hand battles in recorded history!\"\n\nAnd indeed, the two combatants go at each other for page after page....(Jack Kirby must have been in heaven here...I'm sure the plot he and Stan cooked up was simple - \"the pair fight for a long time...\")&nbsp; Even the folks on Yancy Street are paying attention and even try to help Ben - \"we're not lettin' anyone <i>else</i> pulverize our favorite whippin' boy!\"\n\nBut as the fight progresses, the Thing notes that he's growing tired while it seems the Hulk continues to grow ever-stronger!&nbsp; Meanwhile, back at the Baxter Building, Sue has finally found the doctor - he had warned Reed against working so much with the \"strange microbes\" and \"untested formulae\" - he's caught a virus and needs complete rest.&nbsp; Sue says \"Oh, Reed...my darling...I...never realized how much I love you...<i>need</i> you!\"\n\nBack at the battle, the Hulk finally bests Ben and turns his attention back to the city - it's the <i>Avengers</i> he wants only!&nbsp; Ben has been nothing but a minor hindrance.\n\nHowever, the now-abandoned Thing pulls himself up, astonished that he's been beaten, but he'll have none of it!&nbsp; Maybe he needed the pounding to make him realize he's not unbeatable.&nbsp; \"Well, like my dear ol' Aunt Petunia used to say...'You can only die once!' and that's the <i>only</i> way he'll stop me now --- by <i>killin'</i> me!!\"\n\n[CONTINUED]",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "It wasn't the <i>first</i> Thing/Hulk battle (<i>that</i> happened back in [LINK:1962:FF_12|FF #12]), but it was certainly the most <i>extensive</i> one to date!&nbsp; And one look at that cover showing the Hulk absolutely <i>towering</i> over the Thing suggests that the fate of Ben Grimm seems very much in doubt!&nbsp; The so-called \"<b>BATTLE OF THE CENTURY!</b>\" was an [SPAN: severity=success; text=excellent way to attract readers], so kudos to Jack Kirby and company.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=I was hooked... even <i>before</i> I opened the magazine!]\n\n[ASSET: left | AvengersInTheNews | width=19%] As an early Marvel reader (both for real and in [PAGE: scenario] we're now all trapped in, <i>wink</i>), every sign has pointed to something like this happening.&nbsp; The Avengers essentially battled the Hulk over the course of their first three issues (which I wrote about in my [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01] review), and I love the way Stan and Jack <i>immediately</i> tie the events of <i>this</i> issue to what had just happened over in <i>that</i> title just one publication week earlier (which is, of course, the dramatic return of the genuine Captain America in [LINK:1964:AVEN_4|AVEN #4|2027-04-25]).&nbsp; If the cover hadn't been indication enough, the story seems determined to follow up on that ongoing thread, except now in the pages of <i>Fantastic Four</i>!\n\nTo that I say:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Sign me up!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Amazing that I'm already salivating, and I'm still only on the top of page 3!)]\n\n[ASSET: right | NoFormulaForMe | width=19%] What's even more amazing is that, even <i>before</i> Sue reads aloud the newspaper for the benefit of the blind Alicia, the first couple of pages had featured a wary Thing smashing Reed's latest formula aimed at permanently transforming him back to the human Ben Grimm, <i>despite</i> the fact that it would mean the loss of \"one fourth\" of the team's fighting strength.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even amidst the build-up leading to this \"Battle of the Century,\" Stan and Jack don't forget to deliver the characterization -- [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=well done!]\n\nWhat Reed seems to have forgotten, however, is the promise he made to his friend page 10 of last April's [LINK:1963:FF_16|FF #16], where Ben spells it out for him very plainly --\"...forget about tryin' to change me back...\" (and instead, devote his energies to trying to help Alicia see again -- which, to Reed's credit, he at least <i>tries</i> to in [LINK:1963:FF_19|FF #19|2026-12-04]).\n\nWhile it's true that Reed sees the truth -- that Alicia loves <i>Ben Grimm</i>, and not just the rocky Thing (as Ben supposes) --it's likely Stan and Jack who have forgotten this after only nine months -- even if readers (or someone like me) haven't, <i>heh</i>.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I mean, <i>come on</i>!&nbsp; Stan can't even remember Banner's first <i>name</i>, much less what happened in this magazine back in April!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Did I mention that I'm still only on <b>page 3</b>?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Already, I'm rubbing my hands together in anticipation!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | LetYouLive | width=20%] Of course, not everything was sunshine and roses, as that same page features the Hulk hijacking a truck out in New Mexico, demanding a lift, and even threatening its drivers with the line \"Let me ride in your truck and I'll let you live!\"&nbsp; <b><i>Geez!</i></b>\n[BR]\n[BR] I know the Hulk is constantly being hunted, and he certainly has no love of \"puny humans,\" but I don't recall him ever threatening to <i>kill</i> a couple in this manner, [SPAN: severity=error; text=so this strikes me as wildly out of character.]\n[BR]\n[BR] Furthermore, Marvel has already established that the Hulk can cover vast distances simply by making giant leaps, so [SPAN: severity=warning; text=what did he need a lift for in the <i>first</i> place?]&nbsp; This is <i>literally</i> shown shortly afterward, where on page 5, he states that his leg muscles \"push me hard enough to leap for <i>miles</i>!\"\n\nAlong the way, Stan doubles down on the fact that he's forgotten the name of Hulk's alter-ego (perhaps writing this issue right around the same time as last month's [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01]).&nbsp; The Hulk's random transformation back to \"Bob\" Banner likely serves only as a refresher for readers who might not be familiar with the character.\n\nAs shocking as it seems that the writer can't even seem to get the character's name right, putting myself in the context of someone to understand the publishing environment of the time, I can <i>kind of</i> see what Stan and Jack are trying to do, <i>especially</i> given that it's been an entire <i>year</i> since the Hulk had his own magazine.&nbsp; And even though he's appeared in the Avengers more recently, none of those adventures were <i>ever intended</i> to focus solely on the Hulk, so it is still <i>conceivable</i> that some FF readers may not have been familiar with the character.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: As for the \"Bob\" snafu, which started in <i>Avengers</i> #3, Stan's mistake was pointed out to him in an upcoming letters page, and he was forced to retroactively give Dr. Banner the full name of \"Robert Bruce Banner.\"&nbsp; Thus, he saved face in the end, but I'm <i>sure</i> that it was actually just a mistake, <i>heh</i>.]\n\nAnd speaking of mistakes, page 5 has the Avengers out in New Mexico, surveying the damage the Hulk caused to the hijacked truck.&nbsp; There, we find Captain America saying that, \"Only the <i>Hulk</i> could have done this!\"\n[ASSET: center | AvengersAppear | width=large]\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=How on Earth would <i>he</i> know?&nbsp; He's never even <i>met</i> the Hulk!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(something that he's very quick to point out to Rick Jones on page 10 of this month's Avengers issue)]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And <i>curse</i> you, Jack Kirby, for once again showing Iron Man flying with his knees bent!&nbsp; <b><i>Arrgh</i></b><b><i>!</i></b>)]\n\nAgain... I <i>get it</i> -- it's a story, and the intent is to build up to something.&nbsp; In this case, especially given what seems to be coming next month, it's important to show the Avengers out engaging in their favorite pastime -- getting in a little [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Hulk hunting] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(copyright pending)].&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=(Still...)]\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nIndeed, Jack Kirby's battle sequence is largely top-notch, as anyone might expect, with Stan's dialog between the two being also about what you'd expect, so no problems with that aspect of the story.&nbsp; The thing that prevents this issue from rising above just \"Fine\" is the convenient setup of Reed's sudden illness and inability to lead the team into battle.&nbsp; In fact, the story even hints that, had he been there, he could have coordinated their attack to properly subdue the Hulk.&nbsp; But this very sudden illness causes the team to be separated and thus rendered ineffective -- and it seems to be the only reason Ben is thrust out there to do battle alone.\n\n\neader, you knew this was going to happen (as you also know an inevitable Thor/Hulk battle will one day follow -- in the eventual <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> #112), and the \"next issue\" blurb promises that the action is only going to intensify as \"the Avengers join the battle!\"\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nWe admire Ben's courage and tenacity, as he's obviously up against a more powerful opponent than perhaps he's ever had to face, and that's all well and good, but the surrounding circumstances that put him in that position to begin with are suspect and prevent the issue from elevating to a higher status.&nbsp; It remains just \"Fine\" in the end.&nbsp; (But hey, we really <i>do</i> want to make sure we catch that next issue!&nbsp; The Avengers?&nbsp; The Hulk?&nbsp; The Thing?&nbsp; Nice, but we sure as heck hope the actual Fantastic Four make an appearance or two as well!)\n\n\nFinally... <i>um</i>... whatever happened to the Fantasti-Car?&nbsp; Is it still hovering up there above the city?&nbsp; (I guess we're supposed to assume it was programmed to return to the Baxter Building on auto-pilot -- what <i>other</i> explanation is there?)",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * The splash page indicates that this tale is \"double-length.\"&nbsp; Curious, since it seems to be about the same length as any other full-length magazine, so perhaps they meant that the tale was so long, it had to spill over into the next issue.\n\n[HANG0] * Page 5 indicates that the Avengers have arrived in New Mexico in a helicopter.&nbsp; While it's not <i>explicitly</i> stated that they travelled all the way from New York City in the helicopter, the story seems to <i>imply</i> that, but that does lead me to scratch my head.&nbsp; While it's true that the 1964 record of 1,348 miles was set later that year, most helicopters at the time had a maximum range of 200-400 miles.&nbsp; (But then again... they didn't have someone named <b>Tony Stark</b> aboard, <i>heh</i>!)\n\n[HANG0] * The Thing/Hulk battle claimed at least one building, a bus, a Jeep, someone's speed boat and a good bit of one NYC's suspension bridges (the \"Washington bridge\" we find out later), not to mention part of one abandoned building and another abandoned warehouse (lucky all these \"abandoned\" structures seem to litter the streets of NYC!)."
  ],
  "letters_page": "[DEVNOTE: shellhead]\n\nMarc Bailey refers to Iron Man (while discussing <i>Tales of Suspense</i>) as \"Shell Head\" -- in quotes.&nbsp; The response from Marvel asks for \"more respect for our heroes\" and that they don't call him \"shell-head.\"&nbsp; But remember that the Hulk already called Iron Man by that name in [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08], so it's unlikely that Marc Bailey actually coined the name.\n\nRichard O'Baid's letter cracked me up when he pointed out that his doctor, making a house call, was distracted by a copy of [LINK:1963:XMEN_2|XMEN #2|2027-01-17] lying nearby while taking his temperature and indicated \"I'm going to tell my son to start buying it so I can read it too.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=Amazing!]",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}